  user_input {
    user_response: "An answering machine on Android is doable, but Android\'s rules limit how you build it, so the approach matters. Here are the realistic options.\n1. Default dialer / InCallService app (most control)\nYour app becomes the phone\'s default dialer. It can auto-answer after N rings, play a recorded greeting, and save the caller\'s message. The catch is that Android restricts access to the call audio stream. Since Android 10, third-party apps generally can\'t capture the caller\'s voice directly. Some devices allow it, but Google Play policy limits it. Workarounds are to use speakerphone plus the microphone (low quality) or to record through the telephony layer on rooted or custom-ROM devices (not suitable for the Play Store).\n2. CallScreeningService (policy-safe, limited)\nThis lets you screen incoming calls and silence, reject, or flag them, but it can\'t answer them or play audio.\n3. Carrier voicemail with a visual voicemail front end\nThe app doesn\'t answer calls. It fetches and plays voicemails from the carrier\'s voicemail system (IMAP-based visual voicemail where supported). This is the most reliable on Google Play, but it depends on carrier support.\n4. Cloud-based answering (most reliable for recording)\nCalls are forwarded to a VoIP number (Twilio, Africa\'s Talking, etc.). A server plays the greeting, records the message, and optionally transcribes it. The Android app receives push notifications and plays the messages. This avoids Android\'s audio limits, but it costs money per minute and needs a backend.\nSuggested tech stack: Kotlin, Jetpack Compose for the UI, Room for storing messages, and Firebase Cloud Messaging for notifications. Option 4 would also need a small backend (Node.js or Python).\nWhich of these do you want: the on-device answering machine (option 1), or the cloud version (option 4) that records reliably? Once you choose, I can write the project structure and starter code."
    items {
      item {
        text_block {
          content: "---\ntype: \"feature\"\ntitle: \"Build an Android app\"\nicon: \"android_color\"\n---\n\n"
        }
      }
    }
    items {
      text: "An answering machine on Android is doable, but Android\'s rules limit how you build it, so the approach matters. Here are the realistic options.\n1. Default dialer / InCallService app (most control)\nYour app becomes the phone\'s default dialer. It can auto-answer after N rings, play a recorded greeting, and save the caller\'s message. The catch is that Android restricts access to the call audio stream. Since Android 10, third-party apps generally can\'t capture the caller\'s voice directly. Some devices allow it, but Google Play policy limits it. Workarounds are to use speakerphone plus the microphone (low quality) or to record through the telephony layer on rooted or custom-ROM devices (not suitable for the Play Store).\n2. CallScreeningService (policy-safe, limited)\nThis lets you screen incoming calls and silence, reject, or flag them, but it can\'t answer them or play audio.\n3. Carrier voicemail with a visual voicemail front end\nThe app doesn\'t answer calls. It fetches and plays voicemails from the carrier\'s voicemail system (IMAP-based visual voicemail where supported). This is the most reliable on Google Play, but it depends on carrier support.\n4. Cloud-based answering (most reliable for recording)\nCalls are forwarded to a VoIP number (Twilio, Africa\'s Talking, etc.). A server plays the greeting, records the message, and optionally transcribes it. The Android app receives push notifications and plays the messages. This avoids Android\'s audio limits, but it costs money per minute and needs a backend.\nSuggested tech stack: Kotlin, Jetpack Compose for the UI, Room for storing messages, and Firebase Cloud Messaging for notifications. Option 4 would also need a small backend (Node.js or Python).\nWhich of these do you want: the on-device answering machine (option 1), or the cloud version (option 4) that records reliably? Once you choose, I can write the project structure and starter code."
    }
  }
}
