    content: "---\nname: \"android-typography\"\ndescription: >-\n  MANDATORY when adding, customizing, or styling fonts and typography in Android applets.\n  Instructs how to download and install Google Fonts (.ttf) locally using the font-util CLI via run_command instead of using Downloadable Fonts (ui-text-google-fonts).\n---\n\n# Android Applet Typography and Custom Fonts\n\nIn the Android applet environment, **Downloadable Fonts** via\n`androidx.compose.ui:ui-text-google-fonts` (`GoogleFont.Provider`, `GoogleFont`)\nare not supported due to network and container execution constraints at runtime.\nAttempting to use `ui-text-google-fonts` will result in runtime font loading\nfailures or build issues.\n\nInstead, you **must** use the local `font-util` CLI utility via `run_command` to\ndownload static `.ttf` font files from Google Fonts and bundle them directly\ninto your Android app resources (`res/font/`).\n\n## 1. Installing Google Fonts using `font-util`\n\nWhenever you need a custom Google Font (e.g., \"Pacifico\", \"Roboto Mono\", \"Open\nSans\", \"Montserrat\", \"Playfair Display\"), run the `font-util` tool using the\n`run_command` tool:\n\n```bash\nfont-util install \"<FontFamily>\" --target app/src/main/res/font\n```\n\n### Examples:\n\n-   To install Pacifico:\n\n    ```bash\n    font-util install \"Pacifico\" --target app/src/main/res/font\n    ```\n\n-   To install Playfair Display:\n\n    ```bash\n    font-util install \"Playfair Display\" --target app/src/main/res/font\n    ```\n\n### Naming Convention\n\nThe `font-util` utility automatically sanitizes the font family name into valid\nlowercase alphanumeric Android resource syntax (`[a-z0-9_]+.ttf`):\n\n-   `\"Pacifico\"` -> `pacifico.ttf` (referenced in code as `R.font.pacifico`)\n-   `\"Roboto Mono\"` -> `roboto_mono.ttf` (referenced in code as\n    `R.font.roboto_mono`)\n-   `\"Open Sans\"` -> `open_sans.ttf` (referenced in code as `R.font.open_sans`)\n\n## 2. Using Installed Fonts in Jetpack Compose\n\nOnce downloaded into `app/src/main/res/font/`, declare and use the font family\nin your Compose UI using `FontFamily(Font(R.font.<font_name>))` from\n`androidx.compose.ui.text.font.FontFamily` and\n`androidx.compose.ui.text.font.Font`:\n\n```kotlin\npackage com.example.ui.theme\n\nimport androidx.compose.material3.Typography\nimport androidx.compose.ui.text.TextStyle\nimport androidx.compose.ui.text.font.Font\nimport androidx.compose.ui.text.font.FontFamily\nimport androidx.compose.ui.text.font.FontWeight\nimport androidx.compose.ui.unit.sp\nimport com.example.R\n\n// Define the custom FontFamily from local resources\nval PacificoFontFamily = FontFamily(\n    Font(R.font.pacifico, FontWeight.Normal)\n)\n\nval RobotoMonoFontFamily = FontFamily(\n    Font(R.font.roboto_mono, FontWeight.Normal)\n)\n\n// Apply custom fonts in your Material 3 Typography\nval AppTypography = Typography(\n    displayLarge = TextStyle(\n        fontFamily = PacificoFontFamily,\n        fontWeight = FontWeight.Normal,\n        fontSize = 57.sp,\n        lineHeight = 64.sp,\n        letterSpacing = (-0.25).sp\n    ),\n    bodyLarge = TextStyle(\n        fontFamily = RobotoMonoFontFamily,\n        fontWeight = FontWeight.Normal,\n        fontSize = 16.sp,\n        lineHeight = 24.sp,\n        letterSpacing = 0.5.sp\n    )\n)\n```\n\n## 3. Mandatory Constraints & Rules\n\n1.  **Use only standard, local static font dependencies** in `build.gradle.kts`\n    or `libs.versions.toml`. Exclude `androidx.compose.ui:ui-text-google-fonts`.\n2.  **Rely exclusively on `androidx.compose.ui.text.font.Font`** for custom\n    fonts in Kotlin code.\n3.  Always download custom fonts via `font-util install \"<FontFamily>\"` before\n    referencing `R.font.<sanitized_name>` in your Kotlin classes.\n4.  If you need multiple font families for pairing (e.g., a display font for\n    headers and a body font), run `font-util install` for each font family.\n"
