  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Android Components**\n\nI\'m incredibly pleased with how the `android docs fetch` command worked. It was instrumental in acquiring the precise manifest registrations for `InCallService`, `ACTION_DIAL`, `RoleManager.ROLE_DIALER`, `METADATA_IN_CALL_SERVICE_UI`, `METADATA_IN_CALL_SERVICE_RINGING`, and more. This detailed information will be pivotal.\n\n\n**Designing EchoDeck Architecture**\n\nI\'m now deeply engrossed in the architecture of EchoDeck. The integration with Android\'s system components is key. Specifically, I\'m focusing on the `EchoInCallService`, which utilizes `InCallService` for call monitoring and notification display. I\'m carefully considering how to implement a user-friendly auto-answer engine, complete with configurable ring intervals and seamless transition to answering machine functionality.\n\n\n**Implementing Auto-Answer Logic**\n\nI\'m currently fleshing out the auto-answer functionality. I\'ve designed the process: upon answering the call with `VideoProfile.STATE_AUDIO_ONLY`, I\'ll route audio based on speakerphone-capture mode and play the custom greeting (either a pre-recorded `.m4a` file or synthesized text). Then, I\'ll generate an answering machine beep tone and start `MediaRecorder` to capture voicemail, displaying a VU meter and timer.\n\n\n**Finalizing Disconnection Handling**\n\nI\'ve finalized how I handle call disconnections: upon detecting `Call.STATE_DISCONNECTED` or the user stopping the recording, the system finalizes the `.m4a` file, computes its duration, saves a `VoicemailMessage` in **Room**, and then I post a \"New Answering Machine Message\" notification to the user. I\'ve also implemented the `EchoCallScreeningService` for call screening.\n\n\n**Defining Call Control Actions**\n\nI\'m now detailing the user interface controls. I\'ve defined one-tap actions: **Answer Personally**, **Send to Machine Now**, **Speaker**, **Mute**, **Keypad (DTMF)**, and **End Call**. I\'m focusing on the implementation details for the built-in Dialer with `ACTION_DIAL` and `TelecomManager.placeCall`. I\'ll handle `Intent.ACTION_DIAL` with `tel:` URIs using `MainActivity` for the Dialer. I\'ll need to create an interactive dialpad with DTMF feedback and quick call placement. To account for third-party call-stream restrictions, I\'m integrating a real Acoustic Dry-Run Test.\n\n\n**Validating Hardware Acoustics**\n\nMy focus is now on on-device acoustic validation. I\'m building a capture pipeline for a real `.m4a` test message. This lets users test microphone pickup, greeting volume, and speakerphone acoustics directly on the device with a clearly labeled test. It ensures an accurate representation of the hardware\'s audio performance.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nMy current focus is around persistence using **Room** and **DataStore**: The **Room** database will contain `VoicemailMessageEntity` (including waveform peaks!), `GreetingEntity` (with `RECORDED` or `TTS` types), and `ScreeningRuleEntity`. **DataStore** will handle user-specific configuration options like `machineGreetingId`, `autoAnswerEnabled`, `speakerphoneEnabled`, and ring interval settings. I\'m exploring the implications for database migrations and efficient data access.\n\n\n**Refining Preference Management**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `PreferencesRepository` using Kotlin Coroutines and Flow. I\'ve defined the preference keys: `isMachineEnabled`, `ringsBeforeAnswer`, `maxRecordingSeconds`, `speakerphoneCapture`, `playBeepAfterGreeting`, and `screenUnknownCallers`. I\'m focusing on ensuring these preferences seamlessly drive the answering machine\'s behavior and UI. I\'ll utilize DataStore for persistence to support these configurations.\n\n\n**Designing UI Interactions**\n\nI\'m now detailing the user interface and interactions. I\'ll include one-tap \"Grant/Set Default\" buttons that lead directly to Android\'s `RoleManager` or runtime permission prompts for critical permissions like `SCREENING`, `Microphone`, `Phone State`, and `Notifications`. I\'m also planning a Quick Settings strip with a ring interval slider, max message length options, and a speakerphone toggle, with a clear explanation of Android 10+ limitations. I\'ve designed a test button to run an acoustic sequence.\n\n\n**Defining Dialer Features**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the design of the **Greetings** and **Dialer & Rules** components within the app\'s studio interface. I\'m planning to implement a list of saved greetings, including pre-seeded TTS greetings for first-launch functionality. I\'m also including options for users to record custom audio greetings with a VU meter, or create TTS script greetings with a live preview. In the Dialer and Rules section, I\'ll build a tactile dialpad with DTMF feedback, along with a call screening rules manager to allow, auto-answer, or block numbers/patterns.\n\n\n**Updating App Metadata**\n\nI\'m now integrating the generated app icon into the project, ensuring the background color matches the design. I\'m also updating key metadata files like `metadata.json`, `strings.xml`, and `settings.gradle.kts` for consistency. I\'m setting a unique application ID and carefully examining the `build.gradle.kts` file. I\'m carefully reviewing `libs.versions.toml` to confirm the presence of necessary dependencies such as `kotlinx-serialization`, and `androidx.datastore.preferences`.\n\n\n**Evaluating Navigation and Serialization**\n\nI\'m now determining the optimal navigation strategy, evaluating whether to use Navigation Compose with string routes or incorporate `kotlin-serialization`. Guidelines advise against version modifications unless absolutely necessary. After careful assessment, it seems that using standard Navigation Compose string routes with a pre-configured version of `kotlinx-serialization` offers seamless compatibility with existing dependencies, as per the established guidelines.\n\n\n**Refining Dependencies and Metadata**\n\nI\'m currently cleaning up dependencies. I\'ve removed unused libraries such as Firebase BOM, Retrofit, and Moshi to optimize the APK size, according to the build guidelines. I\'ve also integrated the `kotlinx-serialization` plugin, which aligns perfectly with the Kotlin version reference I\'m using. I\'m now updating key files, including `metadata.json`, `strings.xml`, and the Gradle files, and will overwrite the launcher icon assets to use our custom icons.\n\n\n"
